 Even if American farmers did not notice that the nineteenth century had changed the world’s economic relationships, they could see that something was wrong with their own situation. The first sign was the money they received for their crops. From 1883 to 1889, the average price of wheat was 73 cents per bushel, corn 36 cents, and oats 28 cents. In 1890, many grain areas had poor harvests, but people in industrial regions still had money to buy food. As a result, corn and oats prices almost doubled, and wheat rose by 20 percent. Even so, because of shortages—except for corn—the overall return was lower than it had been a year or two earlier. In 1891, abundant crops of wheat, corn, oats, rye, and barley lowered prices for all except wheat and rye, but not back to the 1889 levels.

