As we said, most nitrogen on Earth is in the air as nitrogen gas. Plants and animals cannot use this gas. In the 1900s, scientists discovered how to turn this gas into fertilizers. The method is called the Haber‑Bosch process. Almost all fertilizers that farmers use are made this way.

The Haber‑Bosch process takes place in labs and big factories around the world. It mixes nitrogen gas with hydrogen gas. The gas mixture is then pushed to a very high pressure—about 200 times the normal pressure at sea level. That is the pressure you would feel if you were 2,000 meters (about 6,500 feet) deep in the ocean. The mixture is also heated to a very hot temperature, around 450 degrees Celsius. This work uses a lot of energy. In fact, the process consumes about 1–2% of all the energy used in the world each year.

