Methyl chloride, once hard to make in pure form and expensive, was finally produced cheaply thanks to a new method by Mr. C. Vincent. He discovered that heating a salt made from trimethylamine – a by‑product of beet potash production – breaks it down into trimethylamine, ammonia, and methyl chloride. Washing the mixture with hydrochloric acid removes any leftover base, and the gases are collected over water. The gas can then be dried with sulfuric acid and turned into liquid by applying pressure.

Today, liquid methyl chloride is common. It is mainly used in two ways: first, as a cooling agent, and second, for making dyes from coal tar.

