Augmented reality, or AR, lets you see real places but adds extra computer-made information like sounds, pictures, or maps. It helps you learn more about what you are looking at. AR is a type of technology that makes the real world look different by putting new things on top of it. A similar idea is called mediated reality, where a computer changes what you see, but not always by adding new stuff. In real life we live in the real world. Virtual reality is when the computer creates a whole new world instead of showing the real one. In AR, the added information comes right away and lines up with real objects, like showing sports scores on a TV screen while a game is happening. Modern AR uses computer vision to recognize objects so the information can move with you. This means that facts about the world, like where a radio wave is, can be shown in the exact spot where it is, over the real world.

