 Sometimes, bacteria can swim or float in liquids. They can be in the water from your tap, in juice left on the counter, or even in human blood if a person has a blood infection. Swimming lets bacteria move around to find food or to escape things they don’t like, such as bright sunlight or cells that want to eat them. But most bacteria prefer to stay still. Staying still uses less energy, and bacteria that stay in a good spot can wait for food to come to them.

Staying still is the first step in forming a bacterial biofilm. The first bacterium that sits still may be joined by others, or it may reproduce and create many copies of itself. As more bacteria gather, they produce sticky substances called extracellular polymeric substances. These substances coat the bacteria. The group of bacteria that is coated in this sticky material is called a biofilm. Bacteria in a biofilm feed on food that comes along and can communicate with each other by releasing special molecules. Biofilms are very common.

