After the Qing dynasty ended in 1911, China fell into chaos. Many warlords took control of different parts of the country. Sun Yat‑sen and the Kuomintang (KMT) wanted to stop the warlords and unite China, so they asked other countries for help. No Western democratic nation answered. In 1921, Sun turned to the Soviet Union, which agreed to help only if the smaller Chinese Communist Party (CCP) could join. In 1923 the Soviet Union, the KMT, and the CCP signed the Sun‑Joffe Manifesto, saying the Soviets would help China have one government instead of many. Mikhail Borodin traveled to China in 1923 to help make the KMT more like the Soviet Communist Party. This led to the first united front between the CCP and KMT.

User: You are tasked with rewriting the following text excerpt to be suitable for a 4th-grade reading level. First, you must compute the grade level of the excerpt with the compute_grade_level function. If the text is already at or below the 4th-grade reading level, print 'Excerpt already at or below desired grade level.' and terminate. If the text excerpt is above the 4th-grade reading level, rewrite the text and input it into the compute_grade_level function to check the grade level of the rewritten text. Continue this process iteratively until the grade level of the rewritten text is within one grade level of the desired 4th-grade reading level. And print the last rewritten excerpt preceded by the line 'Rewritten Excerpt:' (no bold or other formatting).
Excerpt:
Tuberculosis (TB) is a very important public health problem. It is estimated that in 2014, 9.6 million people developed TB, and almost 2 million people died of this disease, where India, Indonesia, and China presented almost half of the cases occurring in the world. TB is caused by a bacteria called Mycobacterium tuberculosis that is spread from person to person through the air, when someone with the disease coughs, talks, or sneezes. It is estimated that one third of the people infected with this bacteria do not get sick but still have the bacteria hiding in their bodies, in what doctors call a "latent" (or dormant) state.
There is only one vaccine currently approved for TB in humans, and it is called Mycobacterium bovis Bacillus Calmette–Guérin (BCG). This vaccine has been given to more than 3 billion people since it was developed in 1921, with around 115 million doses per year given to newborns. The BCG vaccine is made from living bacteria that have been weakened in the lab so that they are unable to cause disease. This type of vaccine, using weak but still living bacteria, is called a live attenuated vaccine.

