Model: Rewritten Excerpt:
Tuberculosis, or TB, is a serious illness that many people around the world get. In 2014, about 9.6 million new cases of TB were reported, and nearly 2 million people died from it. India, Indonesia, and China had almost half of all the cases.

TB is caused by a tiny living thing called Mycobacterium tuberculosis. It spreads through the air when a person who has TB coughs, talks, or sneezes. About one‑third of the people who catch the bacteria do not feel sick right away. Doctors call this “latent” or “dormant” TB, because the bacteria are hiding inside the body but not making the person feel ill.

The only vaccine for TB that is officially approved for humans is the BCG vaccine, which is made from a weakened form of a related bacteria called Mycobacterium bovis. It has been used for over a hundred years and has helped protect more than 3 billion people. The BCG vaccine is a live vaccine, meaning it uses bacteria that are alive but too weak to cause disease.

