So, what exactly happens in the brain that makes people react faster to pictures they expect in the activity we described? Scientists study the brain by measuring the tiny electrical waves it makes when we think or act. Brain cells, called neurons, send signals to each other with small electric pulses. These pulses travel through brain tissue and can even be detected on the scalp with electrodes—small metal discs. An amplifier device makes the faint signals stronger so we can see them. This technique is called electroencephalography, or EEG, and the recorded waves are called EEG signals.

An EEG signal comes from the activity of thousands or millions of neurons at once. It mixes many different electrical patterns from various parts of the brain at the same time.

