Machine learning is a part of computer science that helps computers learn from data. In 1959, Arthur Samuel said it means giving computers the power to learn on their own, without being told exactly what to do. The idea is to create programs, called algorithms, that can build a model from examples and then use that model to make predictions or decisions based on new data. These programs do not follow strict instructions; they learn patterns from the data.

Machine learning is closely linked to statistics and math. It uses methods from math to find the best solution. It is useful when we cannot write a detailed program for a task. Some everyday examples are spam filters that block unwanted emails, programs that read printed text and turn it into digital text, search engines that find the right web pages, and computer vision systems that recognize objects in pictures.

