 As for measuring the slope of a water surface, we found that it was a very delicate job. The result of the measurements was not very useful. That would affect the use of all the formulas that needed that data. We measured the water surface by looking at its average oscillations. We found that it was fairly level across, and not convex as some people had said. We had 565 sets of vertical velocity measurements that we combined into forty‑six series. The forty‑six average curves were all very flat and convex downstream—except near an uneven bank—and looked roughly like parabolas with horizontal axes. The data only gave us rough parameters. The line of maximum velocity was usually below the service level and sank in a rectangular channel, from the center outward down to about mid‑depth near the banks. Its depression seemed not to depend on the depth, slope, velocity, or wind. Probably the air itself, being a continuous source of surface retardation, would keep the maximum velocity lower, while wind did not affect this because it lasted only briefly. On any vertical, the mid‑depth velocity was greater than the mean, and the bed velocity was the lowest.

