The Middle Ages was a period of European history that lasted from about 476 CE, when the Western Roman Empire fell, to about 1492, when Christopher Columbus reached the New World. The name “Middle Ages” comes from the fact that it lies between the fall of Rome and the beginning of modern Europe. People also call this time the Medieval Age, the Dark Ages, or the Age of Faith because of the rise of Christianity and Islam. When used narrowly, the term “Dark Ages” refers only to the early part, from 476 to 800 CE, when Charlemagne became king.

After the Roman Empire fell, many towns and cities in Europe were destroyed by invading tribes. Europe was in disarray during this time, and life was difficult. Because few people could write, we know little about the period. The Romans had much knowledge about science, technology, medicine, and literature, but much of it was lost. The Dark Ages were marked by large migrations, wars, and plagues.

