 All things considered, there is no way of keeping apples quite so good and practicable as packing them in light barrels and storing them in cool cellars. The barrel creates a small room inside a room, keeping air from moving around and preventing the fruit from drying and shrinking. It also slows temperature changes. Plus, you can pack more apples in the same space than with any other method. The worst way is using a large open bin. The problems with that are: too many apples touching each other, too much weight on the lower apples, and it is hard to handle and sort them when you need to send them to market. In the past, people in Western New York would sort and barrel the apples right after picking them. They would bring them up the hill and store them in a cool place until winter, then put them in cellars.

