We want to learn about the brain’s short‑term memory. We listen to tiny electric signals in people’s heads while they remember things. We ask them to hold numbers, words, or pictures in mind for a short time, from one second to a minute. These tiny signals show what brain cells, called neurons, do when we remember. The signals change based on how much we remember. Usually we have to remember many things at the same time. For example, while reading this, you remember what you just read. You might also think about what to eat, where to eat, and when you need to be there. Remembering all of this uses signals that go back and forth three to eight times each second. That means some brain cells fire many times in one second.

