 When a mid‑ocean ridge spreads, the seafloor is about 2,600 meters (8,500 feet) deep. On the sides of the ridge, the depth is linked to how old the seafloor is. A simple rule is that depth grows with the square root of age. The shape of ridges is shaped by the balance of weight called isostasy. Near the ridge, hot, light mantle pushes up the crust. As the plate moves away and cools, the mantle gets thicker and heavier. Older seafloor sits on denser material, so it is deeper.

