People often wonder why some kids do better in school. It turns out that doing well depends on special skills called executive functions. These skills help you plan, finish work, and stay calm.

One key skill is working memory. It lets you keep facts in your mind while you solve problems. Another skill is inhibition. It helps you ignore distractions, like not eating all the cookies at once. The third skill is cognitive flexibility. It lets you switch quickly between tasks, such as moving from homework to a short game.

Scientists created simple computer games to test these skills. Kids who play the games well usually do better in class.

