Humans live with a huge group of tiny bacteria inside their stomachs and intestines. These bacteria are called the gut microbiota. Imagine your body as a big team made up of human cells and these bacteria. Most of the cells in that team—about 90%—are bacteria! In this team, some bacteria are like helpers and some are like troublemakers.

The helper bacteria do important jobs. They help break down the food you eat, give you vitamins, keep your immune system strong, and stop germs from making you sick. These helpers usually stay calm with the troublemakers, who are called pathobionts. Pathobionts can cause problems if they become too active.

The balance between helpers and troublemakers in your gut is very important. If the helpers and troublemakers are not in balance, the troublemakers can make you feel sick.

