Model: Rewritten Excerpt:
The brain grows and learns. It makes new links between its parts. These links become a network. A network is a group of parts that work together. Think of it like a band that plays music. The network helps us learn, like reading.

The brain has cells called neurons. Neurons talk by reaching out with tiny arms. They can grow new arms. When many arms connect, they become long paths. These paths let information travel from one part to another.

People use a picture machine to see how the brain works. They find that when we learn to read, different parts of the brain get busy and talk. Some parts have names. One part is the “letter box” where we look at letters and words. Another part helps us match sounds to letters, like seeing that “anana” is part of “banana” when we skip the /b/. A third part is the “captain” that directs the team.

When these parts talk a lot, the paths between them become stronger and easier to use.

