 Assyrian architecture, like the buildings of Babylonia, was shaped by the styles of Sumer and Akkad, and partly by the Mitanni. But soon it gained its own unique look. Palaces had bright wall paintings, and the art of cutting seals—an idea from the Mitanni—became very popular. Schools taught young writers both the Babylonian and Assyrian forms of the Akkadian language. Sumerian and Akkadian books were often copied in the Assyrian style.

Assyrian Akkadian was used in many kinds of documents, from legal contracts to religious prayers, and even in practical manuals on medicine and how to make tools. From the 13th to the 10th century BC, a new kind of picture story appeared. Artists carved long series of pictures on square stone tablets. Like a comic strip, they showed scenes of war or hunting, arranged from the upper left to the lower right, with short notes below each picture. These pictures and the fine seal carvings show that Assyrian art was beginning to outshine Babylonian art. In buildings, a new type of ziggurat was built, with two towers and bright enamel tiles.

