Among all orchid groups, none is as distinct or varied in size, shape, structure, and color as the Masdevallia. The genus was first described almost a hundred years ago by Ruiz and Pavon based on a Mexican species, M. uniflora, which is still little known to modern growers. When Lindley published “Genera and Species” in 1836, only three Masdevallia species were known to scientists. Twenty‑five years later, in his “Folio Orchidaceæ,” about forty species were recorded. Today, about a hundred types are grown in gardens, and travelers report many beautiful ones that live high on the cloud‑covered slopes of the Andes and other New World mountains. Masdevallias are found only in the Western hemisphere. Just as with birds and animals, when a plant group is limited to one hemisphere, those looking for a similar group elsewhere are often disappointed.

