 Japanese records say that the first pottery was made in 660 B.C. But it was not until the Christian era that pottery began to improve significantly. In 1223 A.D. the craft saw major advances in both making and decorating the pots. From then until the 16th century, several famous pottery centers were founded, including Owari, Hizen, Mino, Kioto, Kaga, and Satsuma. The Rahn‑Yaki, a crackled style, was first created in Kioto at the start of the 16th century. The finest old Hizen pots, still the most admired, were produced at Arita Hizen from 1580 to 1585; the early Satsuma pots date from 1592. Consul‑General Van Buren noted that porcelain clay is found in almost all parts of the country, and the different varieties are usually near each other and close to canals and rivers, which is very helpful for transportation.

