Scientists study head injuries by looking at special brain proteins called biomarkers. A biomarker is a sign that a disease is happening. For example, doctors check blood sugar to see how diabetes is doing. For head injuries, important biomarkers are tau protein and a few other brain proteins. These proteins are parts of brain cells that help the brain work, like beams in a building.  

When someone has a head injury, the proteins can break and go into the blood. This makes brain cells weaker and can cause thinking and behavior problems. Let’s talk about tau protein. Neurons are brain cells that have long, thin arms called axons. Axons let neurons talk to each other. Tau helps keep axons strong. If tau builds up, neurons can’t talk properly. This can cause confusion, memory loss, slow movements, and other serious changes.

