 Joel sat upright as best he could, squeezed between a big man and a friendly friend. He tried to keep his tears from falling. Suddenly, a pair of black horses and a big carriage stopped before a little brown house. The doctor was hopping across stepping-stones, running so fast that Joel had to keep up. They arrived at the house, where Grandma Bascom was looking for herbs in the woodshed. Polly was holding David's hand as he lay on a pile of grain bags. The new doctor finished the check quickly. David was sitting up, his head resting on Polly's shoulder. No bones were broken; the only problem was the shock from falling. Polly's cheeks brightened again, and Grandma Bascom said, "Praise the Lord--and who are you, anyway?" She bobbed her hat at the doctor. He laughed and kept her question a secret.

