In this experiment we show how a camera can be used better. First, put the camera close to a wall and have the person sit so that the camera sees about three‑quarters of their face. You will find that you hardly need a reflector. Then take a picture (a negative) and move the camera about five feet farther into the room. Keep the person in the same spot and turn them so the camera sees the same side of their face again. Now you will see that the shadows are much darker. The reflector must be moved closer to light them up, but you can still get soft, balanced light. Take a second picture with the same exposure. If the first picture was taken at the right time, the second picture will be much darker. The person stayed at the same distance from the light source the whole time.

