 He loved his plans. They were a series of colorful stories to him, and he shared them with the world with music. But you would never hear him talk about himself. He put them in front of him, always making sure they were big enough to hide him. When he could stand on his own, he would sneak out at night to look at them, and then if nobody saw him his chest swelled. I have never known anyone more modest, and no one was quite as shy. Many actors have performed for him for years and never talked to him, perhaps seeing him dart into a side street because they were approaching. They might not have known that it was just shyness, but it was. I have seen him forced out of his own theater by people who didn't know him, and he left cheerfully. “Good people, these; they know what they’re doing,” was all he said. Afterwards he was shy about going back, lest they apologize.

